<bold>Conflict of interest:</bold> D.T.Z., M.V., and N.K.K. were investigators of the study. D.T.Z. reports honoraria for lectures from Astra Zeneca, Boehringer Ingelheim, Pfizer, Hemofarm, and Amicus and received support to attend meetings from Boehringer, participates in DSMBs/advisory boards for Boehringer, and is the president of the Serbian Society of Echocardiography. E.M. is a co-founder and scientific advisory board member of Cardiawave and holds shares in the company and subsequently has royalties/licenses, receives consulting fees, support to attend meetings, and is involved in patents. All other authors declare having nothing to disclose.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>7</month><year>2023</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub" iso-8601-date="2023-05-09"><day>09</day><month>5</month><year>2023</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>09</day><month>5</month><year>2023</year></pub-date><volume>24</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage>e108</fpage><lpage>e109</lpage><history><date date-type="corrected-typeset"><day>09</day><month>5</month><year>2023</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2023. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the European Society of Cardiology.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2023</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial License (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ext-link>), which permits non-commercial re-use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. For commercial re-use, please contact journals.permissions@oup.com</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xlink:href="jead089.pdf"/><kwd-group><kwd>aortic stenosis</kwd><kwd>calcification</kwd><kwd>non-invasive ultrasound therapy</kwd><kwd>stroke</kwd><kwd>MRI</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group award-type="grant"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>Cardiawave, Levallois-Perret</institution></institution-wrap>
</funding-source></award-group><award-group award-type="grant"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>European Union&#x02019;s Horizon 2020</institution></institution-wrap>
</funding-source><award-id>829492</award-id></award-group><award-group award-type="grant"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>French Government</institution></institution-wrap>
</funding-source></award-group><award-group award-type="grant"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>National Research Agency</institution></institution-wrap>
</funding-source></award-group></funding-group><counts><page-count count="2"/></counts></article-meta></front><body><p>Transcatheter aortic valve replacement (TAVR) has revolutionized the therapy of aortic stenosis, offering treatment to patients that were previously left untreated. However, even in the TAVR era, around 16% of patients with severe symptomatic aortic stenosis are left untreated due to comorbidities and possible procedural complications such as stroke,<sup><xref rid="jead089-B1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>,<xref rid="jead089-B2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref></sup> calling for less invasive therapies.</p><p>A promising new technology that may cover this treatment gap is non-invasive ultrasound therapy (NIUT). With the novel Valvosoft&#x000ae; device (Cardiawave, Levallois-Perret, France) (<italic toggle="yes">Figure <xref rid="jead089-F1" ref-type="fig">1</xref></italic>), an ultrasound applicator, applied to the patient&#x02019;s chest, delivers focused high-pressure ultrasound pulses with great precision. Where the pulsed cavitational ultrasound beams pass the tissue, there is no effect, but at the focal point, the convergence of multiple beams results in microscopic cavitation bubbles (&#x02018;bubble cloud&#x02019;) that collapse and produce shock waves inducing non-thermal mechanical tissue softening through calcium fragmentation. Ultrasound imaging is used to identify and target the tissue to be treated, and to guide and control the treatment in real-time.<sup><xref rid="jead089-B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref></sup></p><p>The prospective, single-centre Valvosoft Serbian study aimed to confirm the safety of the Valvosoft device observed in the first-in-human study<sup><xref rid="jead089-B3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref></sup> and to rule out silent cerebrovascular events through systematic pre- and post-procedural cerebral diffusion-weighted magnetic resonance imaging (DW-MRI). The study was performed in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and ISO1415 and approved by the locally appointed ethics committee, all patients provided informed consent, and a clinical events committee adjudicated all adverse events. Further details are provided at <ext-link xlink:href="http://www.ClinicalTrials.gov" ext-link-type="uri">www.ClinicalTrials.gov</ext-link> (NCT04665596).</p><p>Patients were eligible if they had severe symptomatic aortic stenosis and were not eligible for TAVR or surgical aortic valve replacement (SAVR). The treatment consisted of a maximum of 60 min of energy applied, divided into sessions of a maximum of 10 min. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) included standard axial and sagittal T2-weighted imaging, axial T1-weighted imaging, coronary T2W fluid-attenuated version recovery sequence, diffusion-weighted imaging with <italic toggle="yes">b</italic> values of 0 and 1000 s/mm<sup>2</sup> and with the calculation of the apparent diffusion coefficient map, as well as high-resolution T2* susceptibility weighted imaging as combined post-processed magnitude and filtered phase images. Following the administration of a paramagnetic contrast agent, imaging was performed in a 3D T1W GRE sequence with multiplanar reconstruction during post-processing.</p><p>The median age of the 10 patients enrolled was 80 years [interquartile range (IQR): 74.5&#x02013;83.0], and patients had multiple comorbidities; 70% had concomitant coronary artery disease, 40% had diabetes mellitus, 40% had renal insufficiency, and 10% had a prior cerebrovascular event.</p><p>Treatment was feasible for all patients with a median treatment duration of 57 min (IQR: 51&#x02013;60 min). Pre- and post-procedural brain MRIs were available for all patients and were performed between 1 and 4 days post-procedure. Acute ischaemic changes were not detected on MRI. Through one month, there was no death, no major adverse event, and no signs of transient ischaemic attack or stroke. Furthermore, there were no clinically significant changes in cardiac markers and the Minimental State Examination values remained stable (<italic toggle="yes">Figure <xref rid="jead089-F1" ref-type="fig">1</xref></italic>).</p><fig position="float" id="jead089-F1" fig-type="figure"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Summary of study outcomes and the mechanism of the Valvosoft device. Valvosoft device (<italic toggle="yes">A</italic>): during treatment, the applicator, which contains an imaging ultrasound and an independent therapeutic transducer, is positioned against the patient&#x02019;s chest, the aortic valve is targeted and imaged using 2-D echo live monitoring (<italic toggle="yes">B</italic>); the treatment aims to soften the valve tissue, improving leaflet mobility and enabling a wider opening of the valve through delivering precise, focused, high-intensity ultrasound (<italic toggle="yes">C</italic>), resulting in the formation of cavitation bubbles that implode, creating local shock waves that mechanically fracture the calcification embedded in the aortic valve without damaging the leaflets or surrounding tissue; the representative diffusion-weighted imaging MRI (<italic toggle="yes">b</italic> = 1000 s/mm&#x000b2;) at baseline (<italic toggle="yes">D</italic>) and post-procedure (<italic toggle="yes">E</italic>) shows that the treatment did not result in any new ischaemic lesions; furthermore, the Minimental State Examination values did not decrease at one month (<italic toggle="yes">F</italic>).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jead089f1" position="float"/></fig><p>The aortic valve area had improved from 0.5 cm&#x000b2; (IQR: 0.4&#x02013;0.7) at baseline to 0.7 cm&#x000b2; (IQR: 0.6&#x02013;0.92) at one month and the mean pressure gradient from 54 mmHg (IQR: 36&#x02013;75) to 38 mmHg (IQR: 31&#x02013;45). The New York Heart Association class decreased by 1.2 classes at one month and the Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire by 22%.</p><p>In summary, in this small cohort, we showed that NIUT is a safe and non-invasive procedure that seems to have no negative measurable cerebral effect. This is important as SAVR and TAVR are associated with a non-neglectable stroke rate and affected neurocognitive function in up to 40% of patients.<sup><xref rid="jead089-B4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref></sup> Moreover, systematic assessments such as in the REFLECT-II study revealed acute post-procedural brain lesions in 85% of TAVR patients, even when treated with a cerebral protection device.<sup><xref rid="jead089-B5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup></p><p>Aside of being non-invasive, NIUT has several other advantages: (i) the concomitant ultrasound imaging allows immediate control of the treatment in real-time, (ii) it allows the immediate reaction to procedural complications, (iii) it requires minimal operator experience, (iv) it does not exclude subsequent SAVR or TAVR, (v) no anaesthesia is required, (vi) it has the potential of ambulatory therapy, and (vii) it leaves no implant behind.</p><p>Based on the outcomes of this study, a European Conformity Mark study was initiated and is currently enrolling (NCT05235568).</p></body><back><ack id="ack1"><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>We thank Radmila Karan, Du&#x00161;ko Terzi&#x00107;, and Vladimir Mili&#x00107;evi&#x00107; for patient recruitment, patient follow-up, and data management, Olga Petrovi&#x00107; for patient treatment and patient follow-up, and Marija Jovanovi&#x00107; for MRI interpretation and data management. Further, we thank Beatrix Doerr, consultant medical writer, for her help in preparing the first draft of the manuscript, reimbursed by Cardiawave.</p></ack><sec id="jead089-s1"><title>Funding</title><p>The study was funded by Cardiawave, Levallois-Perret, France. Cardiawave has received funding from the Horizon 2020 research and innovation program under grant agreement no. 829492 and is supported by the Agence Nationale de la Recherche, France (ANR) under the program &#x02018;Investissements d&#x02019;avenir&#x02019; with the reference ANR-16-RHUS-0003_STOP-AS.</p></sec><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="jead089-s2"><title>Data availability</title><p>The data will be available based on reasonable requests from the corresponding author starting one year after publication.</p></sec><ref-list id="ref1"><title>References</title><ref id="jead089-B1"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal">
